@lang en
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `br-interpretation-time`
IMPORT `br-lindb-life-of-a-law`

-- Tests for br-lindb-life-of-a-law.l4 (arts. 2 to 6). Every expected value was worked out from the text before the file was run.

§ `Art. 2 caput: a law not meant to be temporary lasts until another modifies or revokes it`

-- a law that begins to bind on 24 April 2026, never modified or revoked
`a lasting law with no later law` MEANS
    `A law as art. 2 caput sees it` WITH
        `it was not meant for temporary operation` IS TRUE
      , `the day it begins to bind` IS YMD 2026 4 24
      , `the day a later law that modifies or revokes it begins to bind` IS NOTHING

#ASSERT NOT `the law is in force on` (YMD 2026 4 23) `, as art. 2 caput has it` `a lasting law with no later law`
#ASSERT REFUSED `the law is in force on` (YMD 2026 4 24) `, as art. 2 caput has it` `a lasting law with no later law` BECAUSE "art. 1 gives the day on which a law begins to bind and does not say from what moment of that day"
#ASSERT `the law is in force on` (YMD 2026 4 25) `, as art. 2 caput has it` `a lasting law with no later law`
#ASSERT `the law is in force on` (YMD 2040 1 1) `, as art. 2 caput has it` `a lasting law with no later law`

-- the same law, modified by a later law that begins to bind on 1 September 2026
`a lasting law that a later law modifies on 1 September 2026` MEANS
    `A law as art. 2 caput sees it` WITH
        `it was not meant for temporary operation` IS TRUE
      , `the day it begins to bind` IS YMD 2026 4 24
      , `the day a later law that modifies or revokes it begins to bind` IS JUST (YMD 2026 9 1)

#ASSERT `the law is in force on` (YMD 2026 8 31) `, as art. 2 caput has it` `a lasting law that a later law modifies on 1 September 2026`
#ASSERT REFUSED `the law is in force on` (YMD 2026 9 1) `, as art. 2 caput has it` `a lasting law that a later law modifies on 1 September 2026` BECAUSE "art. 1 gives the day on which a law begins to bind and does not say from what moment of that day"
#ASSERT NOT `the law is in force on` (YMD 2026 9 2) `, as art. 2 caput has it` `a lasting law that a later law modifies on 1 September 2026`
#ASSERT NOT `the law is in force on` (YMD 2026 4 23) `, as art. 2 caput has it` `a lasting law that a later law modifies on 1 September 2026`
#ASSERT REFUSED `the law is in force on` (YMD 2026 4 24) `, as art. 2 caput has it` `a lasting law that a later law modifies on 1 September 2026` BECAUSE "art. 1 gives the day on which a law begins to bind and does not say from what moment of that day"

-- a law meant for temporary operation: before it binds it is not in force; after, art. 2 caput does not say
`a temporary law` MEANS
    `A law as art. 2 caput sees it` WITH
        `it was not meant for temporary operation` IS FALSE
      , `the day it begins to bind` IS YMD 2026 4 24
      , `the day a later law that modifies or revokes it begins to bind` IS NOTHING

#ASSERT NOT `the law is in force on` (YMD 2026 4 23) `, as art. 2 caput has it` `a temporary law`
#ASSERT REFUSED `the law is in force on` (YMD 2026 4 25) `, as art. 2 caput has it` `a temporary law` BECAUSE "art. 2 caput speaks only of a law not meant for temporary operation, and the text does not say when a temporary law ceases"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the law is in force on` (YMD 2030 1 1) `, as art. 2 caput has it` `a temporary law` BECAUSE "art. 2 caput speaks only of a law not meant for temporary operation, and the text does not say when a temporary law ceases"

§ `Art. 2 par. 1 and par. 2: a later law and an earlier one`

-- the pair of laws is built from five answers, in this order:
--   later than the earlier law, expressly declares it revoked, incompatible with it, regulates the whole matter, sets provisions alongside
GIVEN later IS A BOOLEAN
      express IS A BOOLEAN
      incompatible IS A BOOLEAN
      entire IS A BOOLEAN
      alongside IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `How a later law stands to an earlier one`
`a pair of laws where` later express incompatible entire alongside MEANS
    `How a later law stands to an earlier one` WITH
        `it is later than the earlier law` IS later
      , `it expressly declares the earlier law revoked` IS express
      , `it is incompatible with the earlier law` IS incompatible
      , `it regulates entirely the matter the earlier law regulated` IS entire
      , `it establishes general or special provisions alongside those already existing` IS alongside

-- each ground of par. 1 alone revokes
#ASSERT `what art. 2 par. 1 and par. 2 say happens to the earlier law` (`a pair of laws where` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `the earlier law is revoked`
#ASSERT `what art. 2 par. 1 and par. 2 say happens to the earlier law` (`a pair of laws where` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `the earlier law is revoked`
#ASSERT `what art. 2 par. 1 and par. 2 say happens to the earlier law` (`a pair of laws where` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE) EQUALS `the earlier law is revoked`
#ASSERT `what art. 2 par. 1 and par. 2 say happens to the earlier law` (`a pair of laws where` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE) EQUALS `the earlier law is revoked`
-- no ground, nothing alongside: art. 2 lists no ground
#ASSERT `what art. 2 par. 1 and par. 2 say happens to the earlier law` (`a pair of laws where` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `art. 2 lists no ground for revoking the earlier law`
-- par. 2: provisions alongside, and no ground, neither revoke nor modify
#ASSERT `what art. 2 par. 1 and par. 2 say happens to the earlier law` (`a pair of laws where` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE) EQUALS `the earlier law is neither revoked nor modified`
-- a law that is not later has no effect under par. 1 or par. 2, whatever else is true of it
#ASSERT `what art. 2 par. 1 and par. 2 say happens to the earlier law` (`a pair of laws where` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE) EQUALS `art. 2 lists no ground for revoking the earlier law`
#ASSERT `what art. 2 par. 1 and par. 2 say happens to the earlier law` (`a pair of laws where` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE) EQUALS `art. 2 lists no ground for revoking the earlier law`
-- par. 1 and par. 2 together: the text does not say which prevails
#ASSERT REFUSED `what art. 2 par. 1 and par. 2 say happens to the earlier law` (`a pair of laws where` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE) BECAUSE "art. 2 par. 2 says a new law alongside the existing provisions neither revokes nor modifies, par. 1 lists grounds on which a later law revokes, and the text does not say which prevails when both apply"
#ASSERT REFUSED `what art. 2 par. 1 and par. 2 say happens to the earlier law` (`a pair of laws where` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE) BECAUSE "art. 2 par. 2 says a new law alongside the existing provisions neither revokes nor modifies, par. 1 lists grounds on which a later law revokes, and the text does not say which prevails when both apply"
#ASSERT REFUSED `what art. 2 par. 1 and par. 2 say happens to the earlier law` (`a pair of laws where` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE) BECAUSE "art. 2 par. 2 says a new law alongside the existing provisions neither revokes nor modifies, par. 1 lists grounds on which a later law revokes, and the text does not say which prevails when both apply"

-- the grounds of par. 1 on their own
#ASSERT `a ground listed in art. 2 par. 1 exists` (`a pair of laws where` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT `a ground listed in art. 2 par. 1 exists` (`a pair of laws where` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT `a ground listed in art. 2 par. 1 exists` (`a pair of laws where` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT `a ground listed in art. 2 par. 1 exists` (`a pair of laws where` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT `a ground listed in art. 2 par. 1 exists` (`a pair of laws where` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE)

§ `Art. 2 par. 3: a revoked law does not come back`

-- the revoked law is not restored by the revoking law losing force, unless a provision says so
#ASSERT NOT `a revoked law is restored when the revoking law loses force` FALSE
#ASSERT `a revoked law is restored when the revoking law loses force` TRUE

-- the text has no rule on repeal and re-enactment
#ASSERT REFUSED `what was done under a repealed text stands after its re-enactment` (YMD 2026 1 1) (YMD 2026 6 1) BECAUSE "the text has no rule on what a re-enactment does to what was done under the text it re-enacts"
#ASSERT REFUSED `what was done under a repealed text stands after its re-enactment` (YMD 2026 6 1) (YMD 2026 6 1) BECAUSE "the text has no rule on what a re-enactment does to what was done under the text it re-enacts"

§ `Art. 3: ignorance of the law`

#ASSERT NOT `alleging that one does not know the law excuses compliance`

§ `Art. 4: a case on which the law is silent`

#ASSERT count `the sources art. 4 names for a case on which the law is silent` EQUALS 3
#ASSERT `art. 4 names the source` analogy `for a case when the law is silent` TRUE
#ASSERT `art. 4 names the source` `custom` `for a case when the law is silent` TRUE
#ASSERT `art. 4 names the source` `general principles of law` `for a case when the law is silent` TRUE
-- art. 4 says nothing of a case on which the law is not silent
#ASSERT REFUSED `art. 4 names the source` analogy `for a case when the law is silent` FALSE BECAUSE "art. 4 speaks only of a case on which the law is silent and says nothing of the sources for any other case"
#ASSERT REFUSED `art. 4 names the source` `custom` `for a case when the law is silent` FALSE BECAUSE "art. 4 speaks only of a case on which the law is silent and says nothing of the sources for any other case"

§ `Art. 5: the ends the judge attends to`

#ASSERT `the judge applied the law as art. 5 requires` (`How a judge applied the law` WITH `the judge attended to the social ends the law is directed at` IS TRUE, `the judge attended to the demands of the common good` IS TRUE)
#ASSERT NOT `the judge applied the law as art. 5 requires` (`How a judge applied the law` WITH `the judge attended to the social ends the law is directed at` IS TRUE, `the judge attended to the demands of the common good` IS FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT `the judge applied the law as art. 5 requires` (`How a judge applied the law` WITH `the judge attended to the social ends the law is directed at` IS FALSE, `the judge attended to the demands of the common good` IS TRUE)
#ASSERT NOT `the judge applied the law as art. 5 requires` (`How a judge applied the law` WITH `the judge attended to the social ends the law is directed at` IS FALSE, `the judge attended to the demands of the common good` IS FALSE)

§ `Art. 6: what a law in force leaves alone`

-- a thing is built from seven answers, in this order:
--   consummated under the law then in force, exercisable by the holder or for the holder, a term fixed beforehand,
--   a condition established beforehand, that term or condition alterable at another's discretion, a judicial decision, an appeal still lies
GIVEN consummated IS A BOOLEAN
      exercisable IS A BOOLEAN
      term IS A BOOLEAN
      condition IS A BOOLEAN
      alterable IS A BOOLEAN
      judicial IS A BOOLEAN
      appeal IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `A thing a law in force might reach`
`a thing where` consummated exercisable term condition alterable judicial appeal MEANS
    `A thing a law in force might reach` WITH
        `it is an act consummated under the law in force when it was done` IS consummated
      , `its holder, or someone for the holder, can exercise the right` IS exercisable
      , `the start of the exercise of the right has a term fixed beforehand` IS term
      , `the start of the exercise of the right has a condition established beforehand` IS condition
      , `that term or condition can be altered at the discretion of another person` IS alterable
      , `it is a judicial decision` IS judicial
      , `an appeal still lies from it` IS appeal

-- par. 1, the perfect juridical act
#ASSERT `it is a perfect juridical act` (`a thing where` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT `it is a perfect juridical act` (`a thing where` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE)

-- par. 3, res judicata: a judicial decision from which no appeal lies
#ASSERT `it is res judicata` (`a thing where` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT `it is res judicata` (`a thing where` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE)
#ASSERT NOT `it is res judicata` (`a thing where` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT `it is res judicata` (`a thing where` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE)

-- par. 2, the acquired right
#ASSERT `it is an acquired right` (`a thing where` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT `it is an acquired right` (`a thing where` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT `it is an acquired right` (`a thing where` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT `it is an acquired right` (`a thing where` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT `it is an acquired right` (`a thing where` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT `it is an acquired right` (`a thing where` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT `it is an acquired right` (`a thing where` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT `it is an acquired right` (`a thing where` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE)
-- a term fixed beforehand that another can alter: the two readings of "inalteravel" differ, so refused
#ASSERT REFUSED `it is an acquired right` (`a thing where` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE) BECAUSE "art. 6 par. 2 does not say whether 'inalteravel, a arbitrio de outrem' qualifies a term fixed beforehand as well as a condition established beforehand"
#ASSERT REFUSED `it is an acquired right` (`a thing where` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE) BECAUSE "art. 6 par. 2 does not say whether 'inalteravel, a arbitrio de outrem' qualifies a term fixed beforehand as well as a condition established beforehand"
-- but a right its holder can exercise is acquired on both readings
#ASSERT `it is an acquired right` (`a thing where` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE)

-- the caput: what a law in force respects, and so what takes immediate effect
#ASSERT `art. 6 requires a law in force to respect it` (`a thing where` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT `art. 6 requires a law in force to respect it` (`a thing where` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE)
#ASSERT `art. 6 requires a law in force to respect it` (`a thing where` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT `art. 6 requires a law in force to respect it` (`a thing where` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT `a law in force takes immediate effect on` (`a thing where` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT `a law in force takes immediate effect on` (`a thing where` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT `a law in force takes immediate effect on` (`a thing where` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT `a law in force takes immediate effect on` (`a thing where` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT `a law in force takes immediate effect on` (`a thing where` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE)
-- a perfect act or res judicata decides before the refusable acquired-right test is reached
#ASSERT `art. 6 requires a law in force to respect it` (`a thing where` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT `art. 6 requires a law in force to respect it` (`a thing where` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE)
#ASSERT REFUSED `art. 6 requires a law in force to respect it` (`a thing where` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE) BECAUSE "art. 6 par. 2 does not say whether 'inalteravel, a arbitrio de outrem' qualifies a term fixed beforehand as well as a condition established beforehand"
#ASSERT REFUSED `a law in force takes immediate effect on` (`a thing where` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE) BECAUSE "art. 6 par. 2 does not say whether 'inalteravel, a arbitrio de outrem' qualifies a term fixed beforehand as well as a condition established beforehand"
